(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).afoqt=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt.full||[]).push({
  num: 3, part: `Part One: The Posting`, title: `Fine Print`, weeks: `February 8 to 20, 2027`,
  tasks: [`RC`,`SJ`,`WK`,`TR`,`AR`,`AI`],
  cast: [
    [`Rae Sutter`,`Tech sergeant. Full time crew chief on the jet at the end of Kit's row.`],
    [`Gene Albers`,`Supervisor at the Tolliver water plant for twenty two years.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `According to the break room, Kit was already too old.`,
    `She heard it on Monday, February 8, at twenty to eight in the morning, with Boyd's signature folded in her chest pocket. The slip had four empty blocks left. The second one belonged to an office across the street from the hangar that didn't open until eight, so she'd come into the shop to kill twenty minutes with a cup she didn't want.`,
    `On weekdays the shop ran on the full timers. Boyd was on the phone in his office. Two of the young ones were arguing over a parts catalog at the counter. At the table by the window Tech Sergeant Rae Sutter was eating a breakfast burrito out of its foil. She'd crewed the jet at the end of the row since before Kit enlisted, and she knew everybody's business by the simple method of walking the line every morning and asking.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Vern's Nav Table: reading what's there`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Reading Comprehension: answer from the page`,
        body: [`Reading Comprehension is twenty five questions in twenty four minutes on the current Pearson page. The 2015 pamphlet gave more time than that, so check the page again before your date. You get passages written like the reading in a professional military education course, each followed by several questions. Call it a minute a question, and the reading comes out of that minute.`,
          `Read each passage once, at a steady pace, for its shape. What is each paragraph doing? One makes a claim, one gives an example, one answers an objection, one draws the lesson. You don't need to hold the details in your head. You need to know where they live, so you can go back and get them.`,
          `Then answer from the page. Every right answer can be pointed to, either a line that says it or a few lines that together leave no other reading. If you can't put a finger on the support, it isn't the answer, however true it sounds. That's hardest for people who know a lot, because what you know isn't in the passage.`],
        terms: [[`Passage`,`The reading the questions are about. On this subtest, a few paragraphs of formal prose.`],[`Support`,`The line or lines in the passage that make an answer right.`]],
        exam: `Two choices both look right? Go find the line for each. The one you can't point to is usually the trap.` },
      { h: `The kinds of questions`,
        body: [`Most questions are one of a handful of kinds. Main idea asks what the whole passage is for. The right choice covers every paragraph. The traps cover one paragraph well, or claim more than the passage does. Detail wants one line, and its favorite trap is a real detail lifted from some other line. Inference is the thing nobody wrote down that the words still force. Stay close to the text. The right inference is the smallest step the lines force, and a bold leap is usually wrong.`,
          `On word in context, the trap's usually the meaning you'd give the word at the kitchen table. Swap each choice in for the word and hear how the sentence reads. Purpose asks why the author mentioned something: an example, a contrast, an objection to knock down. Attitude asks how the author feels, and prose like this leans mild, so furious or delighted rarely fits. Application asks which new situation fits the passage's principle.`,
          `Here's a short one of my own, so you can hear the kinds at work. Maintenance units keep more written records every decade, and yet the most useful account of a jet on a given morning may still come from the crew chief who worked it overnight. The aircraft forms describe what was done, in the order it was signed. A spoken turnover describes what one person noticed on one shift, a few hours ago: the panel that fought going back on, the reading that was in limits but higher than last week. Neither replaces the other. Crews who launch only from the forms are sometimes surprised. Crews who go only on the turnover may chase a worry that's already been fixed. The careful supervisor takes in both and asks where they disagree.`,
          `Start with the main idea. It isn't that units keep more records. That's the first clause, and it's only there to set up the "and yet." It isn't that the turnover beats the forms either, because the reading says neither one replaces the other. The main idea is the one every sentence serves: a good supervisor uses both the written record and the spoken one, and pays attention where they disagree. Notice the topic sentence comes last here, which happens a lot in this kind of writing.`,
          `Test any main idea choice two ways. If a sentence of the reading would be left out of it, the choice is too narrow. If it says more than any sentence says, it's too big. The best summary is the one that's exactly as big as the whole thing, and on the real test it usually sounds a little dull.`,
          `Now a specific detail. What does a spoken turnover describe? Go back and get the line: what one person noticed on one shift, a few hours ago. The trap choice will say what was done, in the order it was signed. That's a real detail, and it belongs to the forms, lifted from the sentence before. Detail questions are lookups. Point to the line every time, because memory moves details from one sentence to the next.`,
          `A word in context question would ask what given means in "on a given morning." The kitchen table meaning is handed over, like a gift. Swap that in and the sentence falls apart. Swap in particular and it reads fine. So the answer is particular, and the trap is the meaning you'd use anywhere else.`,
          `Then inference. The reading never says one shift's problem can be gone by the next. But "a worry that's already been fixed" leaves no other reading: something the night crew noticed may be settled by launch. That's a fair inference, the smallest step the words force. A choice that says turnovers are unreliable is a leap. The reading calls them useful. The question words tell you when you're on an inference item: suggests that, implies, would most likely agree. When you see them, you get one step past the words and no more.`,
          `Attitude is inference too. What's the author's attitude toward the careful supervisor? Approving, and quietly. Nothing in it is angry at the paperwork or thrilled with crew chiefs. If a choice is implied by the words and modest about it, it's usually right. If it would surprise the writer, it's wrong.`],
        terms: [[`Main idea`,`The claim the whole passage supports. Every paragraph points at it.`],[`Inference`,`Something the passage doesn't state but its lines require.`]],
        exam: `Watch for extreme words in the choices: always, never, only, entirely. Passages in this register tend to hedge, and a choice that doesn't is often wrong.` },
      { h: `The little words`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"A navigator reads a lot of notices to airmen. Most of them don't matter. You find out which one did about an hour after you skipped it."</span>`,
          `Rules, notices and test passages hang their meaning on small words, and those are the words a retelling drops first. May allows. Shall and must require. Unless and except open a door in a rule. Only after puts one thing in front of another. By sets the date a condition gets measured on. Not flips a whole sentence, and at speed it's easy to step right over it.`,
          `When a question turns on a requirement, read the sentence all the way to its period. A sentence that seems to say no often says no unless, and the unless was the part you needed.`],
        terms: [[`Qualifier`,`A small word or phrase that limits a statement, like only, unless, may or by.`]],
        exam: `If a choice is shorter and more certain than the line it came from, check whether it dropped a qualifier on the way.` },
      { h: `Words in a hurry`,
        body: [`Word Knowledge comes earlier on test day, twenty five words in five minutes, but reading feeds it. Read a passage carefully and you've done vocabulary practice too, with the context thrown in for free.`,
          `When a word won't come, try it in a phrase you've heard it in. Waive shows up next to fees. Amend shows up next to rules. Half the time the phrase remembers what the definition can't. The choices on this subtest are often near meanings from the same neighborhood, so pick the one that fits every sentence the word fits, not just the first one you thought of.`],
        terms: [[`Near meaning`,`A wrong choice that's close to the right one, and wrong in one way that matters.`]],
        exam: `Between two near meanings, ask which one you could swap in for the word in any sentence. That's the closest.` },
      { h: `Read your own paperwork`,
        body: [`Your board's announcement is a reading test with your career on it. Read each line to its period: the dates, how your age is measured and on which date, the minimum scores, and whether it sets a PCSM minimum. Some units do, and a PCSM needs a TBAS score, which is its own test.`,
          `An announcement is usually a summary of longer rules. If a line decides whether you can apply, ask your unit or recruiter which rule it comes from, and read that rule's sentence yourself. Rules for pilot applicants change, and this course can't tell you what your unit will use. Be careful with any version that's shorter and more certain than the original, including the one you hear in a break room.`],
        terms: [[`Announcement`,`The unit's posting for a board: who it's for, the dates, the minimums and how to apply.`],[`TBAS`,`A separate computer test for pilot applicants. Its score goes into the PCSM.`]],
        exam: `On the test and off it, the line you can point to beats the version you heard.` },
      { h: `Two equations, two unknowns`,
        body: [`Some word problems give you two things you don't know and two facts that tie them together. That's a system of equations, and you'll see bare ones on Math Knowledge too. Give each unknown a letter, write one equation per fact, and solve the pair together.`,
          `Here's one off a parts order I signed for once, numbers changed. The shop got 12 drums of oil, some 5 gallon and some 55 gallon, 260 gallons in all. Call the small ones s and the big ones b. S plus b is 12. Five s plus 55 b is 260. Use substitution. S is 12 minus b, so put that into the second one. Five times the quantity 12 minus b, plus 55 b, is 260. Sixty plus 50 b is 260. So b is 4, and s is 8.`,
          `The other tool is the elimination method, and it's what I reach for when the numbers line up. Say 3a plus 2b is 16, and a minus 2b is 0. Stack them and add, and the b's wipe each other out. Four a is 16. A is 4, and b is 2. Elimination when a letter already matches across both, substitution when a letter's nearly alone.`,
          `Two traps, and I've stepped in both. One is handing in the letter they didn't ask for. If the item wanted the big drums, 8 is wrong even though every line of your work was right. The other is skipping the check. Put both numbers back into both equations: eight small drums is 40 gallons, four big ones is 220, and 40 and 220 make 260. Ten seconds. It's caught more of my slips than any amount of care going in.`],
        terms: [[`System of equations`,`Two or more equations that share the same unknowns and have to be true at once.`],[`Substitution`,`Solving one equation for a letter and putting that into the other.`]],
        exam: `Before you pick, read the item's last line again and check which letter it asked for.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f3s1`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Before thirty three`, q:`Sutter means it kindly, and the young airmen are listening. What should Kit do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const b=((all||{})[2]||{}).picks||{};return [`Sutter wiped her hands on a napkin and nodded at Kit's chest pocket, where the corner of the slip was showing. ${b[8]===0?`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Heard from Kemp's kids you're leaving us for the front seat."</span>`:b[8]===1?`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Fisk says you told him you weren't going anywhere. Kemp's kids say different."</span>`:`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Word is you put in for the pilot slot."</span>`}`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Boyd signed it yesterday."</span>`,`Sutter looked at her the way you'd look at somebody about to step off a maintenance stand they thought was lower. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Kind, certain, sorry for her.">"Darrow. Somebody ought to tell you before you burn your whole spring on it. You have to be in pilot training before you turn thirty three. That's the rule. My cousin's kid missed it by four months."</span> She folded the foil into a square. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Kind, certain, sorry for her.">"You're done, hon. I'm sorry."</span>`,`The young ones had stopped arguing. Kit could feel the slip against her chest, and the work card behind it with five weeks on the back. She'd read the birthday line herself in January. But a posting was somebody's short version, and Sutter had walked this ramp since before Kit could drive. If the real rule said pilot training, and pilot training was a year out at best, then five weeks was a year and change. A year and change was a wall.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell her the posting says the board date, right there in the break room.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"The posting says the board date, Sergeant. Not pilot training."</span> Sutter didn't argue. She just smiled, the way people smile at a new airman. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Postings are the short version."</span> Kit had nothing to put after that, and the young ones heard her not have it.`,
          why:`You had the page right and the room wrong. Correct somebody senior in front of airmen and she has to defend herself, whatever the page says. And when she came back at you, you didn't know the rest of it yet. Find the rest out before you argue it.`},
        {t:`Ask Boyd to come out and tell Sutter she has it wrong.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Boyd comes as far as his doorway. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"I don't know the age rule, Darrow. I know engines."</span> He goes back to his call. Sutter shrugs at her burrito, and now two people in the shop are sure Kit can't settle her own conversations.`,
          why:`You pulled your flight chief into a conversation between two crew chiefs, over a rule none of you had read. That's borrowing rank to win an argument. The test marks it down, and the shop remembers it longer than the test does.`},
        {t:`Thank her, and say she'll go read what it says before she quits on it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Thanks, Sergeant Sutter. I'm going to go read what it actually says before I quit on it."</span> Sutter shrugged. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Suit yourself. I'd want somebody to tell me."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"You did."</span> At eight Kit walked the slip across the street and handed it over a counter, and she didn't let herself look back at the window.`,
          why:`Integrity and Professionalism. She did you a kindness, as she saw it, so you thanked her for that. You didn't take her word for the rule, and you didn't fight her in front of her airmen. Somebody tells you what a page says? Go read the page.`},
        {t:`Put the slip back in her pocket and drive home.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-4,health:-1},
          after:`She drives home with the slip still in her pocket. By Tuesday she's told herself it was a long shot anyway, and she almost believes it.`,
          why:`You quit on hearsay. Nobody showed you a rule. A woman with a burrito told you about her cousin's kid. Before a secondhand version shuts a door on you, go see whether it's a door.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s2`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`RC`, title:`The birthday line`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a=((all||{})[1]||{}).picks||{};return [`Tuesday, February 9, was her fifth Tuesday at the yellow table. She told Vern about the break room before she had her coat off, all of it, the cousin's kid included.`,`Vern asked what Sergeant Sutter had read. Kit said she didn't think Sutter had read anything. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"And you?"</span>`,`${a[7]===undefined||a[7]===2?`She had the copy the orderly room had printed for her in January, folded in thirds and worn at the creases from riding in her coat.`:a[7]===0?`She had the corkboard copy itself, the one she'd taken down in January, with the pin holes still in the top corner.`:a[7]===1?`The photo on her phone wouldn't do for Vern, so she'd printed both pages off the wing's email that afternoon.`:`She'd printed both pages off the wing's email that afternoon. She hadn't touched the posting since January and still knew most of it by heart.`} She laid page one on the table. Vern put on his reading glasses and read it standing behind her chair, over her shoulder, the way she pictured him reading a chart over a pilot's.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Pilot vacancy, summer 2027 hiring board">The wing will select one applicant for undergraduate pilot training. The selection board convenes Saturday, July 10, 2027.<br>Applicants must hold a bachelor's degree, meet commissioning standards, and have AFOQT scores on file by June 1, 2027. Minimum scores are on page two.<br>Applicants must not have reached their 33rd birthday by the board date.<br>Selection is contingent on a flight physical and on final approval above the wing.</prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Not Sutter's sentence. The paper's. Read me the birthday line to the period, and tell me which date it measures your age on."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`The day she'd start pilot training, the way Sutter said.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Show me where it says pilot training."</span> She looked. It didn't, anywhere on the page. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's Sutter's sentence. I asked for the paper's."</span>`,
          why:`That's the rumor talking, not the page. On the test an answer can sound right and still be nowhere in the passage. If you can't put your finger on the line, it isn't the answer.`},
        {t:`June 1, the day her scores have to be on file.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's a date. It's the wrong line's date."</span> He moved his pencil down one line. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"All the way to the period."</span> She read it again. By the board date. The board date was in the first line.`,
          why:`June 1 is on the page, sure. It's in the wrong sentence. That's a favorite trap: a real detail from the passage, answering a question nobody asked. Read the line the question's about, to the period.`},
        {t:`The day the wing receives her finished package.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Where's that written?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It isn't."</span> Vern waited until she'd looked at the birthday line again, slower.`,
          why:`Nothing on that page says when the wing gets the package. You filled a gap with a guess. Under a clock, a guess can feel a lot like reading. It isn't.`},
        {t:`July 10, the day the board convenes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"July tenth. It says by the board date, and the board date's in the first line."</span> Vern nodded. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's the paper's sentence. Sergeant Sutter has a different one. One of them is the short version."</span> He reached for page two.`,
          why:`The birthday line says by the board date, and the first line says when the board sits. You had to put two sentences together, and both were right there. That's a good share of Reading Comprehension. It also only tells you what this page says. When two versions disagree, the next question is which one came from the source.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s3`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`RC`, title:`Above the wing`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Page two had the minimum scores, in a box.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Pilot vacancy, page two">MINIMUM SCORES<br>AFOQT: Pilot 25. Verbal 15. Quantitative 10. CSO 10, with Pilot and CSO together 50 or higher.<br>PCSM: 10 or higher, on file by the board date.<br>ROUTING<br>Applications begin with the applicant's immediate supervisor.</prop>`,`Kit had read page two in January. She'd read the AFOQT line and stopped there. Vern's pencil went past it to the PCSM line and stayed.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's your Pilot score, your TBAS and your flying hours, mixed by a formula nobody publishes. The TBAS is its own test, on a computer, with a stick. You'll need to book it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I don't have any flying hours."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Then the other two carry it."</span> He waited while she wrote TBAS on the back of page two. Then he turned back to page one and put the pencil under the last line. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Selection is contingent on a flight physical and on final approval above the wing. Tell me what that says about the colonel's yes, and nothing it doesn't."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That even the wing's pick still needs a yes from above it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"The wing picks. Then somebody above the wing says yes or no."</span> Vern tapped the word contingent. She'd had that one in January too, out on the ramp, with Fisk asking what it meant and her not wanting to admit she had to think about it. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"And now?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Depends on."</span> He took the pencil off the page and didn't say anything else, which was about as close as Vern came to telling you you'd done fine.`,
          why:`Contingent means depends on. Final approval above the wing means the wing's choice isn't the last one. That's an inference. Nobody wrote that sentence down for you. You put it together from contingent and from above the wing, and if somebody asked you to show your work, you could point at both. On the test, the right inference is the one you could defend line by line.`},
        {t:`That the colonel, and nobody above her, makes the final call.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Vern read the line back to her, slower than she had, and then asked whether the colonel was above the wing or was the wing. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"She is the wing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Then on this page she isn't the final anything."</span>`,
          why:`Above the wing is above her. You read the colonel into the last word because she's the top of everything you can see from the ramp. The page says otherwise.`},
        {t:`That she has to pass a flight physical before the board sits.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"It says the physical's a condition. Does it say before the board?"</span> She went back over it, and it didn't, not anywhere. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Then don't give it a when. What about the second half?"</span>`,
          why:`The line makes the selection depend on the physical. It doesn't say the physical comes before the board. If anything, contingent means the pick can come first and the physical can take it back. You added a when.`},
        {t:`That the wing can choose someone over the age line.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Where's it say that?"</span> She ran her finger down the page twice, slower the second time, like it might turn up if she gave it a chance. It didn't. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"No. That's the line you wanted to find."</span>`,
          why:`You wanted that one, and the page doesn't have it. When you want an answer bad enough, you'll find it in a passage that never held it. So which line gave you that?`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s4`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The Lieutenant`, q:`Vern flew with the colonel once. What should Kit ask him for?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Vern took his glasses off and folded them, which she'd learned meant he was about to say more than one sentence.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"A posting is the short version. Somebody boiled a long rule down to a page so it would fit on a corkboard. When you asked me at the Riverside, I went and read the long one. Took me two Saturdays on the heritage room computer."</span> He set the glasses on page one. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"It measures you on the date the posting names. Your board date. Sutter's sentence was the rule once, years back. It isn't now. And there's a door in it for somebody just outside the line. An exception to policy. You ask for it in your package, and the unit has to sponsor it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"The unit."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"The wing. The Lieutenant puts her name on it, then it goes up past her, and somebody up there says yes or no. I sat on this wing's boards for a lot of years. The number on that line has moved since my day. Every version I read had a door something like this one."</span> He touched the five weeks she'd penciled in the margin. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"It's written for people a little outside. Five weeks is a little. Next summer you'd be a year and five weeks out, and nobody calls that a little."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, clear, a little stung.">"You've known since January."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Dry, plain, matter-of-fact.">"You hadn't asked."</span> He said it the way he'd said bring a pencil.`,`The wall had a crack in it after all, and a name on the crack. Kit thought of a line of block capitals in a log at home, LT MERRITT, COPILOT, and of the man across the table who'd written it. The thought got as far as her mouth.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask him to call the colonel and put in a word for her.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"No."</span> He let it sit there. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Not because I don't like you, Sergeant. Because she'd have to wonder what else got to her that way. So would you."</span>`,
          why:`You asked an old man to spend his history on you, around your own chain. A back channel is still going over a head, just quieter. Would you want the colonel's yes if you'd had to borrow it?`},
        {t:`Ask him what a request like that needs, and write it herself.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She swallowed the other question. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Careful, steady, holding back.">"What does a request like that need in it?"</span> Vern looked at her a moment, as if he'd heard the one she hadn't asked too. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"A reason somebody can sign under. Your scores, when you have them. And your own words on why."</span> He wrote all three down the margin of page one, one under the other.`,
          why:`Integrity and Professionalism. The request is yours to make, through your chain, in your words. You asked the man across the table how to do it, not to do it for you. That's what a mentor's for. The test keys working a connection to get around a process low, even a friendly one.`},
        {t:`Ask him what the colonel's like, so she can pitch it right.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"She's fair."</span> He put his glasses back on. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's all you get from me. The rest you find out the regular way."</span>`,
          why:`Knowing your audience is fine. Asking for an angle on your commander from someone who flew with her is shopping for a side door. Get ready with your own case, not with her.`},
        {t:`Leave the birthday out of it and let the board do the math.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"The board can count, Sergeant. So can the colonel."</span> He folded his glasses again. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"You want them finding five weeks you didn't mention?"</span>`,
          why:`Leaving out the one fact that decides your eligibility isn't modesty. It's concealment, and the people reading the package will see it. The test keys that among the worst things you can do. Say the hard part first.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s5`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`AI`, title:`Zulu`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Dry, offhand, pointed.">"And I don't call colonels,"</span> he said, to nobody in particular, getting up for the den.`,`He came back with one sheet out of the box and laid it down facing her.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Navigator's log, March 2001" say="My log, seven March, oh one. Tolliver to the track and home, five point eight hours. Weather going down at our alternate, so we picked a new one, a civil field, runway one seven, three five. The notice to airmen said that runway was closed oh four hundred to oh nine hundred Zulu, except with prior permission. The aircraft commander read closed and wanted another field. I read him the whole line. Called ahead and got permission. Didn't need it. Nice to have." by="Vern">07 MAR 01. TOLLIVER, TRACK, TOLLIVER. 5.8 HRS.<br>ALTERNATE WX GOING DOWN. NEW ALTERNATE, CIVIL FIELD, RWY 17/35.<br>NOTAM: RWY CLOSED 0400Z TO 0900Z EXCEPT PPR.<br>AC READ CLOSED, WANTED ANOTHER FIELD. READ HIM THE WHOLE LINE.<br>CALLED AHEAD, GOT PPR. DIDN'T NEED IT. NICE TO HAVE.</prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Except,"</span> Vern said. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Most expensive word on any page, and people quit reading one word before it."</span> He put his finger on the closure times. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Tonight's question is easier. Zero four hundred Zulu. What's the Z doing on the end of it?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Universal time, one clock for every crew on earth.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Universal time. One clock for everybody."</span> Vern wanted to know when the runway had closed on a Tolliver kitchen clock, in early March, six hours behind. She did it on the margin. Ten at night, the evening before. He put the log on top of her posting, which she took to mean it was hers for the week.`,
          why:`Zulu is Coordinated Universal Time, the clock aviation runs on, so a crew in Missouri and a field two time zones away mean the same minute. Tolliver in winter sits six hours behind it, so zero four hundred Zulu is ten the night before. Subtract, and watch the date roll back.`},
        {t:`The time zone of the field that sent the notice.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Then I'd have needed to know every field's clock on the way to the track."</span> He drew a little globe on the legal pad with one line through it.`,
          why:`If every notice ran on its own field's clock, every crew would be doing time zone math on every line. The Z means one clock for all of them.`},
        {t:`Tolliver's own local time, on a twenty four hour clock, like the plant log.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Twenty four hour, yes. Tolliver's, no."</span> He tapped the Z. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"If it were ours, why would they bother with the letter?"</span>`,
          why:`Twenty four hours, yes. Local, no. The Z is the whole point. It's universal time, so nobody has to ask whose four o'clock you meant.`},
        {t:`A closure that repeats every day at that hour.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That'd say daily. In letters."</span> He printed DAILY on the pad in capitals and waited.`,
          why:`The Z is about which clock, not how often. A closure that repeats would say so in words.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s6`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Speed and Spatial`, task:`TR`, title:`Twenty a month`, q:`Which number on the notice is the plant's?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The plant's shift huddle happened at a quarter to seven every morning in the control room, standing up, with the night shift half asleep and the day shift half awake. On Friday, February 12, Gene Albers had a new piece of paper.`,`Gene had run the Tolliver plant for twenty two years and liked his huddles short. He read the top of the notice out loud. <span class="said" data-who="man">"State says we go to eight a month on the distribution samples, starting March. Less driving for whoever has the truck."</span> He tacked it to the board by the door and went on to the filter backwash schedule.`,`Kit was nearest the board. She'd come off ten hours of night shift and was reading it before she'd decided to.`,`<prop type="doc" title="State notice, posted in the plant control room" say="The state notice. Distribution chlorine residual samples, the minimum per month, effective March first. Rows by population served, then two columns, standard and reduced. Five hundred one to thirty three hundred people, four standard, two reduced. Thirty three hundred one to ten thousand, ten and four. Ten thousand one to twenty five thousand, twenty and eight. Twenty five thousand one to fifty thousand, thirty and twelve. The reduced column has an asterisk. A system may move to reduced monitoring only after the department approves it in writing. A system may apply after twelve consecutive months with no distribution sample below point two milligrams per liter free chlorine.">NOTICE TO COMMUNITY WATER SYSTEMS. DISTRIBUTION CHLORINE RESIDUAL SAMPLES, MINIMUM PER MONTH. EFFECTIVE MARCH 1, 2027.<br>POPULATION SERVED · STANDARD · REDUCED*<br>501 to 3,300 · 4 · 2<br>3,301 to 10,000 · 10 · 4<br>10,001 to 25,000 · 20 · 8<br>25,001 to 50,000 · 30 · 12<br>*A system may move to reduced monitoring only after the department approves it in writing. A system may apply after 12 consecutive months with no distribution sample below 0.2 mg/L free chlorine.</prop>`,`The sign out by the road said the plant served about eleven thousand eight hundred people. It had said so since the last census.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Read 8 a month, the reduced column in her row.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She reads eight, Gene's number, and it sits wrong. Right row. The column had a star on its heading, and the star went on for two sentences.`,
          why:`Right row, and a column you haven't earned yet. A heading with a star on it is telling you to read something else before you use it. On a table, the heading is half the answer.`},
        {t:`Read 10 a month, in the second row down.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She reads ten. That's the row for towns up to ten thousand, and the sign by the road says eleven thousand eight hundred.`,
          why:`One row up. Eleven thousand eight hundred is past ten thousand. Ranges have edges, so check which side of the edge you're on before you slide across.`},
        {t:`Read 20 a month, standard column, third row.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Finger on the third row, ten thousand one to twenty five thousand. Finger on the standard column. Twenty. She checked next door both ways, ten above and thirty below, and then the eight beside it, in the column Gene had read. Eight had a star on its heading.`,
          why:`Row first, then column, then the neighbors, so you know what a slip would look like. Twenty, with ten above and thirty below. Table Reading builds many of its wrong answers out of the cells next door. The cell next door here had a star on its column, which is a reading problem of its own.`},
        {t:`Read 30 a month, in the fourth row down.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She reads thirty, one row too far. That's a town twice Tolliver's size, and the plant would be driving the truck all month.`,
          why:`One row down. That's a city twice your size. Anchor on the range before you read across.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s7`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`RC`, title:`The star`, q:`Gene told the huddle eight a month from March 1. What does the notice say has to happen first?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Gene was still on backwash. Kit read the line under the table, the one the star pointed to, and then she read it again, slower.`,`Last August a main had broken on the east side, out past the grain elevator, and for one afternoon a dead end on Cooper Road had read 0.1 on the residual. Kit had pulled that sample herself, in the rain. It was in the log in her own handwriting.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Twelve straight months with no sample under 0.2.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She has the twelve months, and she stops there. That's the footnote's second sentence. The first one says only after the department approves it in writing, and nobody had asked the department anything.`,
          why:`You read one gate and stopped. The footnote has two sentences, and the first is the one that says no. When a passage stacks conditions, the question usually wants all of them.`},
        {t:`A letter to the state before March 1, asking for it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She goes looking for the deadline and can't find it. The notice says a system may apply. It never says by when. She'd made one up to fill the gap.`,
          why:`There's no deadline in that footnote. You filled a gap with something that sounded official. Reading questions punish that, and so do state inspectors.`},
        {t:`Nothing at all; it starts March 1 for everybody.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She reads it the way Gene did, the top line and the table and the date. The star sits there under all of it, saying otherwise in two whole sentences.`,
          why:`Effective March 1 is when the notice starts, not when everybody gets the reduced number. The asterisk was the rule. A star on a heading is the page telling you there's more.`},
        {t:`A clean year, then the state's okay in writing.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Two things, in order. A year with nothing under 0.2, and then the state saying yes on paper. Tolliver's clean year couldn't even start until after Cooper Road, and nobody had asked the state for anything. Twenty a month, then, for months yet.`,
          why:`Only after is a gate. May apply after twelve months is a second gate in front of the first one. Both are qualifiers, the small words a summary loses first. Gene read the headline and the table and the date, and every one of those reads was right. He just stopped above the star.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s8`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`After the huddle`, q:`Gene misread the notice in front of both shifts. How should Kit handle it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Gene got to the bottom of his list, looked around the room over his reading glasses, and asked what he asked every morning. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Anything else?"</span>`,`Twelve people in the control room, half of them already reaching for coats. The day operator who had the truck this month was grinning about all the driving he wouldn't be doing. Gene stood by the door under his own notice, the way he'd stood under every notice for twenty two years. He was a good supervisor. On March 1 he was going to cut the plant's sampling by more than half, on account of a star he hadn't read.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Speak up now and read the footnote out to the whole huddle.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She reads the footnote out loud. She's right, and everybody hears that she's right. Gene takes the notice down, reads it, and says <span class="said" data-who="man">"Noted"</span> in a voice that closes the subject. At the next huddle he doesn't ask if there's anything else.`,
          why:`You had the page and the right answer. You also had both shifts watching, and a supervisor who now gets to be wrong in front of all of them. A public correction is rarely the worst move on the test, and it's rarely the best. Same facts, his office, five minutes later.`},
        {t:`Let the huddle break up, then take the notice to Gene alone.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She let the coats go on and the room empty, then took the notice off the board and knocked on Gene's open door. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Gene. The reduced column has a star on it."</span> She laid it on his desk with her thumb under the footnote. He read it, then read it again with his finger. He looked at the ceiling a while. <span class="said" data-who="man" data-tone="Slow, rueful, remembering.">"Cooper Road. Last August."</span> She nodded, and he picked up a pen. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Twenty a month, then. I'll fix it at tomorrow's huddle myself. Where'd you get so particular about footnotes?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Homework."</span>`,
          why:`Communication, with some respect in it. The mistake was his, so he got to see it first and fix it himself, in front of the same people who'd heard it. You brought the page, not an opinion, and you pointed at the line. That's how you correct a boss: privately, early, with the evidence in your hand.`},
        {t:`Email the city's utilities director with the notice attached.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The director forwards it to Gene before noon with one line, please advise. Gene fixes the schedule. He doesn't say a word to Kit for the rest of the month, which at a twelve person plant is a long time.`,
          why:`Going over a head is the move the test most often keys least effective. He'd have fixed it in five minutes if you'd shown him. Instead he heard it from his boss, with your name on the forward.`},
        {t:`Pull twenty a month on her own shifts and say nothing.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She pulls her share of twenty on her shifts. The other shifts pull eight between them, the way Gene said. In April the monthly report goes to the state short, with her careful numbers inside it.`,
          why:`Doing it right on your own shifts while the plant does it wrong isn't a fix. It hides the problem until the state finds it. Quietly working around somebody's mistake is a pick the test keys low.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s9`, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`Twelve weeks`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On Saturday morning Graham had the whiteboard off its bracket and flat on the kitchen table, with the week wiped clean.`,`He'd drawn twelve narrow columns across it in his lab quiz printing, one for every week from Monday the fifteenth through Sunday, May 9. In each column, in green, VERN, two hours on Tuesday. Then four one hour blocks in blue, wherever her plant shifts and his lab nights left a hole: two on weekdays, and one each on Saturday and Sunday morning. Over three of the columns he'd drawn his cartoon tanker, the drill weekends, and under each tanker he'd crossed out both weekend blocks. His own week was on there too, in black and smaller: his lab nights, and nothing else. The Saturday mornings that used to say HARDWARE STORE, or nothing, were blue now. Hers.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"And the age thing?"</span> he said, still drawing.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Offhand, quick, hopeful.">"There's a waiver. Vern says the wing has to sponsor it. The colonel."</span>`,`Graham nodded at the marker. In the bottom corner he wrote MAY 15 with a question mark, the Saturday he'd picked, two weeks clear of June 1 in case the scores were slow. Past that the whiteboard just ended at its aluminum edge, and he didn't write anything there. She saw where the board stopped. She didn't ask what went on the other side of it, and he didn't say.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Pop quiz,"</span> he said, capping the green. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"No calculator. How many hours does that give you, from the fifteenth through the ninth?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Seventy two, six for each of the twelve weeks.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Graham tapped one of the cartoon tankers with the cap. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Those cost you."</span> She did it over.`,
          why:`Seventy two is the plan with no exceptions in it. The exceptions were drawn right on the board, with a tanker over each one. Arithmetic Reasoning loves a rule with a few exceptions. Do the rule, then take out what the exceptions cost.`},
        {t:`Sixty six, two off each drill week.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Sixty six. Six a week is seventy two, and the three drill weeks lose two apiece."</span> Graham wrote 66 under MAY 15 and drew a box around it, and then a second box around the first, which was how she knew he was pleased.`,
          why:`Seventy two less three times two. Or nine ordinary weeks at six, fifty four, plus three drill weeks at four, twelve. Sixty six either way. Pick the method you'll get right at minute twenty eight of twenty nine.`},
        {t:`Sixty, taking four off each drill week.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"I only crossed out two a weekend."</span> He showed her the blue X's, two under each tanker, and she counted them herself.`,
          why:`Each drill weekend kills two one hour blocks, Saturday's and Sunday's. That's two hours a drill week, not four. Count what's actually crossed out.`},
        {t:`Fifty four, leaving the drill weeks out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Vern doesn't take drill weekends off. Neither do the weekdays."</span> He tapped a green VERN under one of the tankers.`,
          why:`You threw away whole weeks. Only the weekend blocks went. The Tuesday and the two weekday blocks in those weeks still count.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s10`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The fridge`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On Sunday, February 14, Kit drove across the river to her mother's. Rhonda had made a Valentine's supper every year since Kit was in kindergarten, pot roast and a heart shaped cake that never came out of the pan in one piece, and Graham was coming over once he'd finished grading.`,`The fridge in that kitchen had been collecting paper since before Kit could reach the handle. The family day picture was still on the freezer door, held up by a magnet from the dentist. Somebody had clipped February's lunch menu over the top half of it, so what showed was a seven year old in a seat too big for her, looking up at something hidden behind Tuesday's chili. Kit could have unclipped the menu. She knew what was under it, a hand pointing up, and she left it where it was, the same way she'd left the box on Vern's table alone.`,`Rhonda kept her back turned and her hands in the gravy. <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda" data-tone="Warm, worried, a little hurt.">"Darlene on my line says her nephew in the fuel shop says you're going to fly jets. And that you're too old, so you can't. Kitty, which is it, and why am I hearing it from Darlene?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say she should have heard it from her, then tell her all of it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, sorry.">"You should've heard it from me. I'm sorry, Mom."</span> Rhonda turned around then, spoon and all. So Kit told her: the board in July, her own birthday in June, the page that said board date where the break room said pilot training, and the door in the rule with the colonel's name on it. Not 2019. Her mother had 2019 the way the whole town had it, Boyd and his inspection, and Kit left that where it was too. Rhonda didn't interrupt once, which for her was close to a record. When it was done she pointed the spoon at her. <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda">"So you're not too old."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Dry, wry, plain.">"I'm too old unless."</span> Rhonda put the spoon down on the stove and glanced at the freezer door, at the lunch menu. <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda" data-tone="Soft, fond, remembering.">"You were always looking up at something, Kitty."</span>`,
          why:`Communication. The people close to you get it from you, first and straight, not secondhand off a cafeteria line. You owned that she found out the wrong way, and then you fixed the version she had. The test likes that order. Own the miss, then give the facts.`},
        {t:`Ask who in the fuel shop is talking, so she can set them straight.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Rhonda">"So you can do what, go yell at a boy in the fuel shop?"</span> She stirred harder. <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda">"I asked you which it is."</span>`,
          why:`She asked you a question, and you went looking for somebody to blame for the gossip. The talk wasn't the problem. She heard it from Darlene because she didn't hear it from you.`},
        {t:`Tell her it's a long shot, so there's nothing to worry about yet.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Rhonda turned around with the spoon. <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda">"Kitty. I've been your mother thirty two years. When you say there's nothing to worry about, that's when I start."</span> She went back to the gravy, and they talked about Darlene's hip instead.`,
          why:`Kind, and it keeps her out. She asked a straight question, and you handed her a reason not to ask again. Worry with the facts in it is smaller than worry without them.`},
        {t:`Tell her the wing wants somebody to try, and she's the one they asked.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Rhonda">"The wing asked you."</span> Her mother looked at her a long moment, the way she used to look at report cards. <span class="said" data-who="Rhonda">"All right, Kitty."</span> She didn't believe it, and they both knew she didn't.`,
          why:`That's the story where somebody else wanted it. It's a lie, a small one, and your mother has been catching your small ones since you were four. The test keys dressing up the truth low, even when it's meant to spare someone.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s11`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`RC`, title:`Seven circles`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She'd written the letter of intent on Sunday night after supper and Monday at the plant, at the break table, between feed checks. The posting asked for one page. She'd made it fit.`,`On Tuesday, February 16, Vern read it with his pencil out. He didn't correct anything. He drew a small neat circle around two words, then around the same two words again further down, and kept going.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Letter of intent, first draft" by="Kit" say="Letter of intent, first draft. I am applying for the wing's pilot training vacancy on the summer 2027 board. I have served in this wing for more than eleven years, and since 2018 I have been the dedicated crew chief on one of the wing's K C one thirty fives. In that time I have trained airmen for the wing and learned its aircraft from the ground up. The wing will convert to a new aircraft in the coming years. I believe the wing would be well served by a pilot who has crewed its jets and knows what it takes to launch them. As an officer I would continue to train airmen and support the wing's maintenance. My goal is to give back to the wing that has given me so much.">I am applying for the wing's pilot training vacancy on the summer 2027 board.<br>I have served in this wing for more than eleven years, and since 2018 I have been the dedicated crew chief on one of the wing's KC-135s. In that time I have trained airmen for the wing and learned its aircraft from the ground up.<br>The wing will convert to a new aircraft in the coming years. I believe the wing would be well served by a pilot who has crewed its jets and knows what it takes to launch them.<br>As an officer I would continue to train airmen and support the wing's maintenance.<br>My goal is to give back to the wing that has given me so much.</prop>`,`There was no shortage of I in it. I am applying, I have served, I believe. She'd checked. Each I was hauling a load for somebody else.`,`Vern slid it back across. Seven circles. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Read it the way a board reads it, with twenty of these in a stack in front of them. One sentence. What's it about?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That she's wanted to fly for as long as she can remember.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Where?"</span> She read it top to bottom. It wasn't there. Not one word of it was there.`,
          why:`That's the letter you meant to write. It isn't the one on the table. A main idea question asks what's on the page, and outside knowledge is a trap, even when the knowledge is about yourself.`},
        {t:`That she's crewed the wing's jets for more than eleven years.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's in it. Second paragraph. Is it what the letter's for?"</span> Kit said it was more what held the rest up. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Then go find what it's holding up, Sergeant."</span>`,
          why:`True, and it's a supporting detail. The main idea is the claim the details are there to hold up. Ask what the eleven years are evidence for.`},
        {t:`That the wing's new jets will need more pilots than it has.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's not in there either. You've got the wing wanting a pilot. You never say how many."</span>`,
          why:`You went one step past the page. It says the wing would be well served, not that the wing is short of pilots. An inference has to stay inside what the words support.`},
        {t:`That the wing would be well served by a pilot who crewed its jets.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, deflated.">"It's about the wing."</span> Out loud it sounded worse than it had on paper. <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, deflated.">"A pilot for the wing."</span> Vern went down the circles with the tip of his pencil, tap, tap, tap, the whole page. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Seven. Main idea's whatever a page keeps coming back to. Yours keeps coming back to us."</span>`,
          why:`Main idea is what the whole page keeps coming back to, and seven circles is a lot of coming back. On the test the main idea choice tends to cover every paragraph, not just the strongest one. Every paragraph here pointed at the wing. Where was the crew chief who wrote it?`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s12`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Who it's from`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Vern took the letter back and turned it face down, so the circles were on the table and the words weren't.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"It's a fine letter for the wing to write about you, Sergeant. The board can get that one from Boyd. So who's this one from?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him a board wants to hear what she'd bring the wing.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"It does, in a paragraph, and you've got five of them. Somebody has to bring the you."</span>`,
          why:`Half right. A board does want to know what you'd bring. It also wants to know why you, and only you can write that half. Answering the easy part of a question is still dodging it.`},
        {t:`Ask him to mark it up the way he'd write it himself.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Then it'd be my letter, Sergeant, and they didn't post my birthday."</span>`,
          why:`That hands your mentor the one job that's only yours. The test scores doing somebody else's work for them low, and letting him do yours is that same mistake, just sitting in the other chair.`},
        {t:`Tell him the truth: she doesn't know how to write the other one.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, honest, exposed.">"I don't know how to write the other one."</span> It was the truest thing she'd said at that table in five weeks of Tuesdays, and Vern, to his credit, didn't make a thing of it. <span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Plain, easy, unhurried.">"Nobody does, the first time. Don't write the letter, then. Write me one sentence with you in it. Just the one. Bring it Tuesday."</span>`,
          why:`Integrity and Professionalism, and some nerve. You told your mentor exactly where you're stuck, without dressing it up. That's the only thing a teacher can work with, and it's worth more than a better draft.`},
        {t:`Tell him she'll swap the wing for the unit and bring it back.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Then I'll circle the unit, and we'll be right back here next Tuesday."</span>`,
          why:`Trading one word for another just fixes the spelling of the problem. The letter would still be about everybody but you. Change what it says, not what it calls things.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f3s7`, title:`Small words`, intro:`Gene's notice turned on two small words, once Kit looked. Here are six more lines. Pick what the word in capitals does.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Operators MAY take a second sample if the first is cloudy.`, choices:[`Allows it`,`Requires it`,`Forbids it`], answer:0, why:`May gives permission. Nobody has to take the second sample.`},
      {prompt:`Each shift SHALL log the residual before handover.`, choices:[`Suggests it`,`Requires it`,`Allows it`], answer:1, why:`Shall is a requirement, the same as must. Skipping it breaks the rule.`},
      {prompt:`The gate stays locked UNLESS a supervisor is on site.`, choices:[`Names the rule`,`Names the penalty`,`Names the exception`], answer:2, why:`Unless introduces the case where the rule doesn't hold. The rule is the locked gate.`},
      {prompt:`Reduced sampling begins ONLY AFTER written approval.`, choices:[`Sets what has to come first`,`Sets a deadline`,`Offers a choice`], answer:0, why:`Only after is a gate. The approval comes first, and nothing in the line says when.`},
      {prompt:`Applicants must not have reached their 33rd birthday BY the board date.`, choices:[`The day the work is due`,`The day the condition is checked`,`The day applications open`], answer:1, why:`By names the date the age gets measured on. It's the yardstick, not a due date.`},
      {prompt:`Residuals USUALLY drop at the far end of the system.`, choices:[`States a tendency`,`States a rule`,`States a requirement`], answer:0, why:`Usually is a hedge. It says what tends to happen, and it leaves room for the exceptions.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Bright lines`, text:[
      `Every organization that hands out something scarce, whether admission, promotion or a seat in a training class, has to decide who qualifies, and it usually does so with a line. Applicants must be under a certain age, over a certain score, or inside a certain window of dates. A line of this kind has real virtues. Anyone can read it, it costs almost nothing to administer, and it treats the hundredth applicant exactly as it treated the first. It also spares the institution an argument it would rather not have, since an applicant who falls short has been measured rather than judged. Nobody has to trust the judgment of the person holding the clipboard, because the clipboard holds no judgment.`,
      `The cost shows up at the edges. A line drawn at a round number is a generalization about a whole population, and it fits the middle of that population far better than its margins. The applicant who misses an age limit by a month is not meaningfully different from the one who meets it by a month, yet the line sorts them as though they were, and it does so with the same confidence it brings to cases that are nowhere near close. Rule writers are generally aware of this. Few of them can remedy it by moving the line, however, since a new line has new edges of its own, and the applicants just beyond it will have exactly the complaint their predecessors had.`,
      `What many of them do instead is add a door. An exception process lets a case near the line be judged on its merits rather than on its birthday. The door is usually narrow by design. It tends to require that someone with authority ask for the exception on the applicant's behalf, and that someone more senior still approve it. That arrangement does more work than it appears to. It attaches a name to the decision. A line can't be blamed for anything. A person who sponsors an exception can be, and knows it, and that knowledge tends to make sponsors careful in a way no written criterion could.`,
      `This is also why exception processes resist becoming routine. If every case near the line were approved, the line would in effect have moved, and the edges would reappear one step further out. A sponsor who signs too readily also spends the credibility that made the signature worth having, and the next request bearing that name is read with more suspicion. So the process asks more of the applicant near the line than of the one comfortably inside it: a stronger record, a clearer reason, a case persuasive enough that the sponsor is willing to be associated with it in writing. The applicant inside the line need only qualify. The applicant outside it must also be worth the risk.`,
      `Applicants tend to read only the line, and that is understandable, since the line is what gets posted. But a posted line is usually the summary of a longer rule, written for readers who will never consult the original, and the longer rule is where the doors are kept. The summary is also the version most likely to be repeated, so many of the people who discuss a rule have never seen the part of it written for the hardest cases. Someone who stops reading at the line may mistake a door for a wall.`] },
    p2: { title:`The fix`, text:[
      `Before satellites, a navigator over an ocean at night knew where the aircraft was in two ways and trusted neither one completely. The first was dead reckoning. From a known starting point, the navigator applied the heading flown, the speed through the air and the time elapsed, corrected for the best available estimate of the wind, and plotted where the aircraft ought to be. The method needed nothing from outside the aircraft, which was its great advantage: it worked above cloud, in turbulence, and on a night with no moon. Its weakness was that it was always a little wrong. A wind forecast off by a few knots, a heading held a degree to one side, a clock read late: each error was small, and each one kept growing for as long as the aircraft flew on the same assumptions. After several hours an honest navigator drew the dead reckoning position not as a point but as a widening circle.`,
      `The second way was the fix. With a sextant, the navigator measured the angle of a star above the horizon and noted the exact time of the observation. The time mattered as much as the angle, since the stars wheel overhead and a clock four seconds off could move the result about a mile. Published tables turned that angle into a line on the chart, somewhere along which the aircraft had to be. One star gave one line, which was useful but not a position. Two stars gave a crossing. Three gave a small triangle, and a careful navigator learned to read the size of the triangle as a report card on the shots themselves. A tight triangle meant the work was good. A large one meant something, a star misidentified or a time misread, had gone wrong, and the sound response was to shoot again rather than to split the difference.`,
      `The two methods were not rivals. A fix couldn't be taken continuously. It took minutes of work and a clear sky, and it described where the aircraft had been at the moment of the observations, not where it was by the time the arithmetic was done. Dead reckoning carried the aircraft forward between fixes, and from the last fix to the present minute. Each new fix, in turn, wiped out the error dead reckoning had collected since the one before and gave it a fresh place to start. Dead reckoning returned the favor: a navigator who already knew roughly where the aircraft was knew which stars to look for and where, and didn't waste a clear patch of sky puzzling over which bright point was which. Neither method alone could have crossed an ocean with much confidence; together they made the crossing routine. A navigator who skipped a fix because the dead reckoning looked reasonable was trusting an estimate at exactly the moment it had become least trustworthy.`,
      `The habit outlived the instruments. Crews flying with satellite navigation are still taught to cross check it against an independent source, because a single system that is usually right gives no warning on the day it isn't, and a crew that has stopped checking is the last to find out. The navigators who flew by sextant would have recognized the principle at once. A position is only as good as the last time it was checked against something that didn't share its errors.`] },
    p3: { title:`Secondhand`, text:[
      `Psychologists who study rumor have run versions of the same simple experiment for nearly a century. One person reads a short account and tells it to a second, who tells it to a third, and so on down a line of half a dozen people. Then the last version is compared with the first. The results are consistent. The story gets shorter. A few details survive and grow sharper, often the vivid or surprising ones. And what remains drifts toward what the tellers already expected to hear, so that an unusual event comes out the far end looking more ordinary than it was.`,
      `None of the tellers is lying. Each passes along what seemed to matter, in words that seemed natural. The losses happen in the choosing. Qualifications are the first thing to go, because they're hard to remember and dull to repeat. A road that is closed at night except to residents becomes, within two tellings, a road that is closed.`,
      `Organizations are long lines of tellers. A requirement written carefully at a headquarters is summarized in a briefing, the briefing is summarized in an email, and the email is summarized aloud at a shift change. Every step is taken in good faith by someone trying to save the next person time. By the time the requirement reaches the people who have to act on it, it may have lost exactly the clause that mattered to them.`,
      `The remedy isn't to stop summarizing. No one could get through a working day without summaries. It's to keep the original within reach and to treat any summary as a pointer back to it. When a decision turns on a requirement, the person making the decision should read the requirement itself, at least the sentence that applies, and should be wary of any version that is shorter and more certain than the original. Certainty is cheap in a retelling. The qualifiers are what cost something to keep.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which of the following best states the main point of the passage?`, passage:`p1`, opts:[`Eligibility lines should give way to judging every applicant on individual merit.`,`Applicants who miss a line by a small margin ought to be admitted automatically.`,`Lines are cheap and fair in the middle but crude at the edges, where exceptions help.`,`Sponsors who approve exceptions usually act against their organization's interest.`,`Age is a poor way to decide who should qualify for positions that are scarce.`], a:2, why:`Main idea. The passage grants the line its virtues, shows where it fails, and offers the exception process as the answer to that failure. The first and second choices go further than it does, and nothing in it says sponsors work against their organizations.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`As used in the first paragraph, "the clipboard holds no judgment" most nearly means that`, passage:`p1`, opts:[`the person applying the line has nothing left to decide`,`officials who apply lines are often poorly trained`,`written records are less reliable than memory`,`applicants can't see the criteria used on them`,`decisions made with a line are rarely reviewed`], a:0, why:`Phrase in context. The sentence before it says nobody has to trust the judgment of the person holding the clipboard. With a line, there's no judgment left to trust, because the line decides. Nothing in the passage questions anyone's training or the reliability of records.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`The author would most likely agree that raising an age line by one year would`, passage:`p1`, opts:[`remove the unfairness the passage describes`,`make sponsors more willing to sign requests`,`reduce the number of applicants overall`,`make an exception process unnecessary`,`create a new group just outside the line`], a:4, why:`Inference. The second paragraph says rule writers can't fix the edge problem by moving the line, since a new line has new edges of its own. Moving it doesn't remove the unfairness or the need for a door. It moves both.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`The author says that "a line can't be blamed for anything" mainly to`, passage:`p1`, opts:[`excuse officials who apply the rules strictly`,`show why requiring a sponsor adds accountability`,`argue that people should replace lines entirely`,`suggest applicants have nobody to appeal to`,`point out that sponsors are often blamed unfairly`], a:1, why:`Purpose of a detail. The sentence comes right after the point that an exception puts a name on the decision. The contrast is the reason for it: a line answers to no one, and a sponsor does. The passage never says sponsors are blamed unfairly.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`According to the passage, an applicant just outside a line who seeks an exception should expect to`, passage:`p1`, opts:[`meet the same standard as applicants inside it`,`be approved once any sponsor can be found`,`see the line itself moved for the next round`,`need a stronger case than those inside it`,`send the request straight to the very top`], a:3, why:`Detail. The fourth paragraph says the process asks more of the applicant near the line than of one comfortably inside it: a better record, a clearer reason. It also says a sponsor who signs too easily loses credibility, so finding any sponsor isn't the end of it.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`The passage is mainly concerned with`, passage:`p2`, opts:[`explaining why satellites took over from the navigator's sextant`,`how two imperfect ways of finding position were used together`,`the training that navigators received in the years before satellites`,`why dead reckoning was more trustworthy than a celestial fix`,`the particular dangers of crossing an ocean at night`], a:1, why:`Main idea. Every paragraph is about the two methods and how each covered the other's weakness, and the last one carries the idea into the present. Satellites appear only at the end, and the passage never ranks dead reckoning above the fix.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`According to the passage, a large triangle formed by three star lines suggested that`, passage:`p2`, opts:[`the aircraft was flying faster than planned`,`the wind forecast had been badly wrong`,`the aircraft had wandered off its course`,`a mistake had been made with the shots`,`dead reckoning should be trusted instead`], a:3, why:`Detail. The second paragraph says a tight triangle meant good work and a large one meant something had gone wrong, like a misidentified star or a misread time. Wind and course belong to dead reckoning, which the triangle doesn't measure.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`It can be inferred from the passage that dead reckoning is least accurate`, passage:`p2`, opts:[`just before a new fix is taken`,`immediately after a fix is plotted`,`at the start of a flight from a known point`,`whenever the sky is clear enough for stars`,`when the navigator shoots three stars`], a:0, why:`Inference. Dead reckoning errors keep growing as the aircraft flies on, and each fix wipes them out. So the error is biggest right before the next fix, which is the point about the navigator who skips one. Right after a fix, or from a known start, it's smallest.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`As used in the third paragraph, "rivals" most nearly means`, passage:`p2`, opts:[`enemies`,`equals`,`partners`,`imitations`,`competitors`], a:4, why:`Word in context. The paragraph says the methods weren't rivals and then shows them working together, so rivals here means competing alternatives. Enemies is too strong for two methods, and partners is what the paragraph says they were instead.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`Which of the following best fits the principle in the last paragraph?`, passage:`p2`, opts:[`An operator reads the same analyzer twice to be sure of its number.`,`A pilot holds the heading the autopilot was given before takeoff.`,`An operator checks an online analyzer against a separate hand test.`,`A clerk copies a figure from one report into a second report.`,`A mechanic replaces a gauge before it has a chance to fail.`], a:2, why:`Application. The principle is to check a reading against something that doesn't share its errors. A hand test with its own kit is independent of the analyzer. Reading the same analyzer twice repeats any error it has, and so does copying a figure from one report to another.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?`, passage:`p3`, opts:[`Retellings tend to drop qualifiers, so decisions should rest on the source.`,`Most people who pass rumors along are trying on purpose to mislead.`,`Organizations should stop using summaries when they make decisions.`,`The vivid details in an account are usually its most reliable ones.`,`Experiments on how rumors spread have produced inconsistent results.`], a:0, why:`Main idea. The passage moves from the experiments to organizations to a remedy: keep the original and read the sentence that applies. It says plainly that nobody could work without summaries, so the third choice overshoots, and it says none of the tellers is lying.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`According to the passage, which part of an account tends to disappear first?`, passage:`p3`, opts:[`its most vivid or surprising details`,`the names of the people involved`,`the qualifications on what was said`,`the parts that seemed most ordinary`,`the conclusion it was building toward`], a:2, why:`Detail. The second paragraph says qualifications are the first thing to go, because they're hard to remember and dull to repeat. The vivid details are the ones that survive and get sharper.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`The author would most likely treat a summary that is shorter and more certain than its source as`, passage:`p3`, opts:[`proof that the source was badly written`,`an improvement worth passing along`,`evidence that someone set out to lie`,`a reason to ban summaries altogether`,`a signal to go back and read the source`], a:4, why:`Inference. The last paragraph says to be wary of any version shorter and more certain than the original, and to keep the original within reach. A signal to check the source follows from that. The author says nobody in the chain is lying and that summaries are necessary.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`As used in the last paragraph, "cheap" most nearly means`, passage:`p3`, opts:[`inexpensive`,`poorly made`,`dishonest`,`easy to get`,`stingy`], a:3, why:`Word in context. Certainty is cheap in a retelling because dropping a qualifier costs the teller nothing. The next sentence sets it against the qualifiers, which cost something to keep. Inexpensive is the everyday meaning, and the passage isn't talking about money.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`The author mentions a road "closed at night except to residents" in order to`, passage:`p3`, opts:[`argue that road closures are poorly announced`,`show how a qualifier can drop out in retelling`,`give an example of a vivid detail growing sharper`,`show that tellers usually lie about the details`,`suggest that residents are being treated unfairly`], a:1, why:`Purpose. The example comes right after the claim that qualifications go first, and it shows except to residents vanishing within two tellings. It isn't an example of a detail sharpening, and the paragraph opens by saying none of the tellers is lying.`, task:`RC`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`STIPULATE: closest in meaning`, opts:[`suggest`,`negotiate`,`propose`,`specify`,`assume`], a:3, why:`To stipulate is to specify something as a condition, as when a contract stipulates the delivery date. Propose and suggest are softer, offering a term instead of setting one. Negotiate is how terms get worked out, not the act of naming them.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`PRECLUDE: closest in meaning`, opts:[`prevent`,`postpone`,`discourage`,`predict`,`precede`], a:0, why:`To preclude is to prevent, to rule something out ahead of time. Discourage and postpone are the near misses: one makes a thing less likely and the other moves it later, but neither rules it out. Predict and precede share the pre and nothing else.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`TACIT: closest in meaning`, opts:[`informal`,`reluctant`,`unspoken`,`polite`,`gradual`], a:2, why:`Tacit means understood without being said, like a tacit agreement nobody wrote down. Informal is close in feel, since tacit agreements usually are, but a written note can be informal too. Reluctant and polite describe how someone acts, not whether a thing was said.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`If 2x + 3y = 13 and x + y = 5, what is the value of x?`, opts:[`3`,`2`,`5`,`1`,`4`], a:1, why:`Double the second equation to get 2x + 2y = 10, then take it away from the first. The x's drop out and y is 3. Then x is 5 minus 3, which is 2. If you picked 3, you found y and stopped. They asked for x.`, task:`MK`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`PRECURSOR: closest in meaning`, opts:[`follower`,`warning`,`cursor`,`successor`,`forerunner`], a:4, why:`The prefix pre is before and the root curs is run, as in current, so a precursor is one that runs before: a forerunner. Cursor shares that root, since a cursor is literally a runner, but it names the runner, not the one that comes first. A precursor can be a sign of what's coming, so warning is the near miss. But a precursor is the earlier thing itself, and forerunner says that exactly. Follower and successor come after, the opposite.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const p=s.picks||{};return [
    `On Saturday, February 20, Graham went to bed at ten with half a stack of chemistry quizzes still ungraded on the counter, and Kit stayed up with the laptop. She had Vern's copy out too, the one he'd circled. The twelve weeks were back up on the fridge in green and blue.`,
    `${p[11]===undefined||p[11]===2?`One sentence, Vern had said. Just the one.`:`Seven circles, and not one of them was around her.`} She left his circles alone and went to the bottom, to the line about giving back to the wing that had given her so much. She highlighted it and hit delete. Now the bottom of the page was empty except for the cursor, blinking where the sentence used to be.`,
    `For twenty minutes she typed things and took them back out. A couple were about the jet. One was about Boyd, and she got rid of that one fast. What stayed was four words.`,
    `I want to fly.`,
    `Four words, on a page that had cost her two nights. She read them the way Vern had taught her, all the way to the period. Not one of them was carrying anything for anybody else.`,
    `The printer lived in the spare room on top of a box of Graham's old lab manuals. She got as far as the print window. Copies, 1. Under the floor the furnace kicked on, ran a while and quit, and she was still looking at the button.`,
    `She hit Cancel. She saved it as LETTER 2, right beside LETTER, and shut the laptop on both.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch03.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Integrity`
});
